From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ps5cemu <ps5cemu@localhost>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 00:22:53 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] PS5: timers on the kernel's clock again

The TSC timer (877b770) is taken back: with it, Breath of the Wild ran in
the 40s where it held 59-60 frames per second at 1440p before, and
HighResolutionTimer paces Cemu's frame timing. Its frequency came from
sceKernelGetTscFrequency, which was not checked against the counter's
real rate. HighResolutionTimer counts the monotonic clock's nanoseconds
again, as in 1.0.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 .../highresolutiontimer/HighResolutionTimer.cpp     | 13 -------------
 1 file changed, 13 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/util/highresolutiontimer/HighResolutionTimer.cpp b/src/util/highresolutiontimer/HighResolutionTimer.cpp
index 507434d..54fa4fb 100644
--- a/src/util/highresolutiontimer/HighResolutionTimer.cpp
+++ b/src/util/highresolutiontimer/HighResolutionTimer.cpp
@@ -1,10 +1,5 @@
 #include "util/highresolutiontimer/HighResolutionTimer.h"
 
-#if defined(CEMU_PS5)
-#include <x86intrin.h>
-extern "C" uint64_t sceKernelGetTscFrequency();
-#endif
-
 HighResolutionTimer HighResolutionTimer::now()
 {
 #if BOOST_OS_WINDOWS
@@ -18,11 +13,6 @@ HighResolutionTimer HighResolutionTimer::now()
     return HighResolutionTimer(nsec);
 #elif BOOST_OS_MACOS
 	return HighResolutionTimer(clock_gettime_nsec_np(CLOCK_MONOTONIC_RAW));
-#elif defined(CEMU_PS5)
-	// The TSC, as Windows' performance counter is on most machines. The PS5's clock_gettime is a
-	// call into the kernel, and Cemu reads the time often: the GPU thread on each turn of its
-	// command loop while it waits for work.
-	return HighResolutionTimer(__rdtsc());
 #elif BOOST_OS_BSD
     timespec pc;
     clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC, &pc);
@@ -43,9 +33,6 @@ uint64 HighResolutionTimer::m_freq = []() -> uint64 {
 	return (uint64)(freq.QuadPart);
 #elif BOOST_OS_MACOS
 	return 1000000000;
-#elif defined(CEMU_PS5)
-	// the console's own figure (1.6 GHz), which the kernel has measured
-	return sceKernelGetTscFrequency();
 #elif BOOST_OS_BSD
 	// now() counts nanoseconds, whatever the clock's resolution: FreeBSD's clock_getres gives
 	// that as 10^9 / the clock's frequency + 1 nanoseconds, so with a clock of 1 GHz or less
